course: biology
lesson: 8
title: The ocean: open sea, sunlit zone and the deep dark
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The ocean is a building of floors made of water: the sunlit zone in the top 200 metres, where there is enough light for plankton to grow, and below it ever darker floors down to the black deep, where the only light is made by animals themselves.
explain: When you snorkel the water looks blue because water soaks up red light first and blue light last, so the deeper you go the bluer and darker it gets, until there is no sunlight at all.
explain: A drop of seawater under the school microscope wriggles with plankton, tiny drifting algae and animals; they are the bottom of the ocean food chain, and the tiny algae among them make about half the oxygen we breathe.
explain: In Jaws one shark terrorises a whole town, but real shark attacks are very rare, most of the 500 or so kinds of shark are smaller than a person, and people catch tens of millions of sharks every year.
explain: In Finding Nemo the anglerfish that chases Marlin and Dory is a good likeness: a big female with a glowing lure on her head, and a real male anglerfish of some kinds is tiny and fuses onto the female for life.
fact: The sunlit zone is the top 200 metres of the ocean, where there is enough light for plant-like plankton to grow, and ocean plankton make about half the oxygen in our air.
fact: Plankton are tiny drifting living things, such as algae, baby fish and copepods; many swim up to the surface at night and sink by day, the biggest daily journey of animals on Earth.
fact: The whale shark is the biggest fish, often 12 metres long or more, but it filters plankton and small fish from the water, and each one has its own pattern of spots.
fact: Sharks are fish with skeletons of cartilage; there are over 500 kinds, most smaller than a person, they have lived for over 400 million years, and attacks on people are very rare.
fact: Deep-sea anglerfish lure prey with a light made by bacteria living in the lure, and in some kinds the tiny male fuses onto the much larger female for life.
fact: Giant squid have eyes up to about 27 cm across, among the largest of any animal, and a giant squid was first filmed alive in its deep home only in 2012.
fact: An octopus has no bones, so it can squeeze through any gap bigger than its hard beak; it has 3 hearts, 8 arms and blue blood, and most of its nerve cells are in its arms.
fact: Sperm whales dive over 1,000 metres deep, sometimes over 2,000, hunting squid by echolocation clicks, the loudest sounds made by any animal, and they have the biggest brain of any animal.
fact: Female sea turtles cross oceans to lay eggs on the beach where they hatched, probably finding it partly by sensing Earth's magnetic field, and warmer sand gives more female babies.
fact: Sea turtles eat jellyfish and can mistake floating plastic bags for them; plastic can block the stomach so the turtle feels full and starves, and tiny microplastics have been found from the deepest trenches to Arctic ice.
fact: The Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench is about 11 km deep, with pressure over 1,000 times that at the surface, and Everest put inside it would have its peak about 2 km under water; yet small fish, shrimp-like animals and microbes still live down there.
fact: Flying fish escape tuna and other hunters by leaping out and gliding on wing-like fins, sometimes over 200 metres, beating their tails on the water to stay up longer.
fact: A squid has 8 arms and 2 longer tentacles, and like an octopus it has 3 hearts.
exercise: What is the sunlit zone of the ocean? | The top 200 metres, where there is enough light for plankton to grow | The sandy beach where people sunbathe and swim | The deepest part, where volcanoes glow | The layer of water just under the ice
exercise: Why does the sea get bluer and darker as you dive deeper? | Water soaks up red light first and blue light last | The deep water is dyed blue by fish | The sky's reflection gets stronger deeper down | Deep water is made of a different liquid
exercise: How much of the oxygen in our air do ocean plankton make? | About half | Almost none | All of it | About one hundredth
exercise: What are plankton? | Tiny living things that drift with the currents | Big fish that live in the deep | Seaweeds that grow on rocks by the shore | Grains of sand floating in the sea
exercise: What does the whale shark, the biggest fish, eat? | Tiny plankton and small fish filtered from the water | Seals and dolphins caught with its huge teeth | Other sharks | Seaweed from the seabed
exercise: What is a shark's skeleton made of? | Cartilage, like the bendy part of your ear | Bone, like a cod's or a salmon's | Shell, like a crab's | It has no skeleton at all, like a jellyfish
exercise: How long have sharks lived in the oceans? | Over 400 million years, before the dinosaurs | About 10,000 years, since the last ice age ended | Since the first ships sailed | About 1 million years
exercise: What makes the light of an anglerfish's lure? | Bacteria living inside the lure | A tiny natural battery in its head | Sunlight it stored during the day | Electricity made by its sharp teeth
exercise: Why do giant squid have such huge eyes? | To catch what little light there is in the deep | To scare away the sperm whales that hunt them | To see the sun from far below | To find their way home by the stars at night
exercise: How can an octopus squeeze through a tiny gap? | It has no bones, only a hard beak | It can fold its bones flat like a fan | It melts into liquid for a short moment | It sheds its skin to get much smaller
exercise: How many hearts has an octopus? | 3 | 1 | 2 | 8, one for each arm
exercise: How does a sperm whale find squid in the dark deep? | By echolocation: it clicks and listens for echoes | By the glow of its own skin | By smell, like a shark | By feeling the water with its long, thick whiskers
exercise: How do sea turtles find the beach where they hatched? | Scientists think they sense Earth's magnetic field | They follow the lighthouses shining along the coast at night | They follow the smell of chips from the cafés on the beach | They swim behind older turtles that already know the way
exercise: What decides whether baby sea turtles are boys or girls? | The temperature of the sand: warmer gives more females | The size of the egg | The colour of the beach | The mother chooses for each egg as she lays it
exercise: Why are plastic bags so dangerous for sea turtles? | Turtles mistake them for jellyfish, and the plastic blocks their stomachs | Turtles get stuck inside them and float away | The bags make the sea too hot | The bags scare away the turtles' food, so they starve
exercise: How deep is the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench? | About 11 km | About 1 km | About 100 km | About 500 metres
exercise: Why do flying fish leap out of the water? | To escape fast hunters like tuna | To catch the flies buzzing above the waves | To dry their wet fins in the warm sun | To breathe in fresh air for a little while
test: «jaws was real basically, sharks hunt people» What is right? | Attacks are very rare; people kill far more sharks than sharks kill people | Yes, every shark hunts people | Yes, but only in summer, when lots of people swim in the sea and sharks follow the smell of suncream | No, sharks are vegetarian
test: «biggest fish = scariest teeth, has to be» What is right? | The biggest fish, the whale shark, filters tiny plankton | Yes, the whale shark eats seals and dolphins with its huge teeth | The biggest fish is the blue whale | The biggest fish is the great white
test: Count them: two octopuses and a squid in the aquarium. How many hearts? | 9 | 3 | 6 | 4
test: Count them: an octopus and a squid side by side. How many arms and tentacles altogether? | 18 | 16 | 20 | 10
test: In Finding Nemo the anglerfish has a glowing lure on its head. Which part is right? | The lure does glow, lit by bacteria living inside it | Anglerfish live in sunny shallow reefs | The light comes from a swallowed lightbulb that still works | Anglerfish lure prey with a song
test: What would happen to Everest if you dropped it into the Challenger Deep? | Its peak would still be about 2 km under water | Its peak would stick out of the sea by about 2 km | It would fill the trench right up to the top and spill over | It would float back up
test: «nothing could live down the mariana trench, pressure'd squash it» What is right? | Some fish, shrimp-like animals and microbes still live there | Nothing at all lives below 1 km | Only giant squid live there | Only plants can live there, as animals would be squashed flat by the water
test: «sea's massive, a few plastic bags won't do anything» Which is the best answer? | Turtles eat bags like jellyfish, and microplastics are found everywhere | Plastic melts in seawater in a day | Fish eat plastic and grow bigger | Plastic sinks to the bottom of the sea and never harms anything that lives down there
test: «octopus has 8 legs and one heart like everything else» What is right? | It has 8 arms, 3 hearts and blue blood | It has 10 legs, 2 hearts and green blood | It has 8 legs, no heart at all and clear blood | It has 6 arms, 3 hearts and bright red blood
test: «why's the sea blue down there, is it the sky» Which is the best answer? | Water soaks up red light first, so blue is what is left deeper down | It reflects the sky even 20 metres down | Fish paint the water blue | Salt makes the water blue, and the deeper you go the more salt there is
test: «sea's just water and fish, who cares about plankton» What is the clever answer? | Plankton feed the whole ocean and make about half our oxygen | Plankton are just dirt in the water | Plankton only feed whales | Plankton make the sea salty, which is why we cannot drink seawater
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sunlit zone of the ocean? | The sunlit zone comprises the uppermost two hundred metres of the ocean, wherein light suffices for the growth of plant-like plankton | The sunlit zone comprises the deepest region of the ocean, wherein the glow of submarine volcanoes furnishes light for the growth of plankton | The sunlit zone comprises solely the intertidal shore | The sunlit zone comprises the water beneath the polar ice
exam: Identify the source of the luminescence emitted by the lure of the deep-sea anglerfish. | Bacteria resident within the lure | Sunlight absorbed in shallow water | An electrical organ within the jaw | Reflection of the light of other fishes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the anatomy of the octopus. | The octopus possesses three hearts, eight arms and blue blood, and lacks any bone | The octopus possesses a single heart and a skeleton of bone, by which its eight arms are supported | The octopus possesses ten arms and red blood | The octopus possesses two hearts and a shell
exam: Ascertain the consequence for a sea turtle of the ingestion of a floating plastic bag. | The plastic may obstruct the stomach and occasion a false sense of fullness, whereby the turtle starves | The plastic is digested without consequence | The plastic renders the turtle buoyant and swifter, whereby it more readily escapes from those predators which pursue it | The plastic augments the nourishment of the turtle
